r here is we want the  soil to form a ball  this is the first part of the the test  if it will form a ball  okay but there we go we're pretty close  a little more  no you're just about there when your  hands are  covered in dirt  okay now reading through our flow chart  uh the first questions it's  it'll ask is will will the  sample form a ball uh when squeezed  right so i'm going to give it a little  squeeze and it retains its shape  if it then wants to just kind of shatter  and fall apart  following through the diagnostic we end  up with that your sample is sand  so this is not just sand uh we move on  okay so the the next uh  part of this test is to try to form a  ribbon and  the ability of the soil sample to form a  ribbon and hold  it is going to be a measure of the  percentage of clay  okay so  to form a ribbon you can take the sample  in your  palm of your hand like this and you  start to kind of squeeze  and feed it out and you want  it to break naturally and fall okay to  the table  there it goes you do that a couple times  and each time  estimating the length so here we have  about three quarters of an inch  put it back into a ball form  it's dried out a little bit in my hand  feed it a little more water we don't  want water to be  squeezed out  okay going back to forming a ribbon  squeezing it out and  fell this time we hit about an inch okay  and we'll just do one more ribbon  and then we'll take the average of the  three  okay there it goes and it shattered when  it hit but  just under an inch okay so now  following through our our flowchart here  diagram  i'm reading yes we have a ball  and and the ribbon formation  we had an inch or less so we're in the  lone category right so the next  uh part of the test is to estimate the  percentage of  grit or sand in the in the sample so i'm  going to take  a little bit of it put the rest aside  about a teaspoon or two here  i'm gonna  wet it in the palm of my hand  and this process is intended to kind of  flatten it out  estimate the percentage of sand  as a factor of how gritty the soil  sample feels  okay so the the diagram asks us does the  sample feel very gritty equally gritty  and smooth  or very smooth so  kind of just got to give an estimation  here  it's somewhat gritty and somewhat smooth  it's kind of feels somewhere in the  middle  okay so the diagnostic has led us to  the conclusion then that this sample is  alone so i'm going to listen to the  sample  and see if i can hear the sound of the  grit rubbing  yeah and i can hear it just a little bit  it's somewhere between  a loam and a sandy loam then  and so if we turn to the texture  triangle we can actually then convert  that  diagnosis into percentages of  the various particle sizes right so  reading here  we have a sandy loam  being composed of something around 20  clay max and  somewhere between uh 70 to  50 sand  and a loam being as low as 30  sand so we're probably  somewhere down in that 30 percent to 40  range  below sandy loam yeah okay so let's try  the next sample  by appearance alone there's not really  much we can tell  they there's kind of  there's no way to visually distinguish  these two soils  in terms of their texture here but by  running through the soil texture by feel  we should be able to make some  conclusions  so make sure we get all the last sample  off  okay and again we're going to sort the  sample a little bit  moving some roots  so we have a sufficient quantity there  to get our ribbon  and wetting the soil  a little more it's still not quite  consolidating  just a little bit more  okay yeah that's starting to  form a little film on my hands so we  should be there you don't want to over  over saturate it okay going back to our  flowchart the first uh diagnostic  is to see if the sample will form a ball  and here we have a nice ball so  and under a little pressure from the  finger it doesn't fall apart doesn't  shatter  so we know we don't have a sand sample  as we suspected  and give it a little squeeze still con  stays together stays put so it's it's  not a sand or a loamy sand  so we're gonna move down into uh the  next step which is the ribbon forming  process  and get  teed up in my hand right and start  feeding it out  and right away i can tell that it's  forming a longer ribbon  this one broke when it hit but there we  have maybe an inch and a quarter  run that three times  i'm just gonna let it naturally just  feed over my forefinger  until it wants to fall okay  all right there may be almost two inches  and one last sample  all right maybe an inch and a half  so about an inch and a half average and  just under that  uh so reading across uh  we've gotten into the clay loam category  okay so now we need to estimate the  amount of grit in the sample so i'm  going to take a small portion of it  in the palm of my hand  and re-wet it in order to form a film  which i get a sense of the percentage  here  recalling that this relates back to the  percentages  of the various particles in the texture  triangle  okay already i can tell there's  some grit there certainly but  i'm not doing as much as the previous  sample at all  and i'm listening  there's a little little bit of sound of  friction but  not a lot certainly less than half  so we're more on the smooth side with  this one  reading down somewhere between  clay loam and a silty clay loam  so let's see going over here to the  texture triangle  finding a clay loam here and a silty  clay loam here  we can tell then that we're you know  somewhere between  uh 30 to 40 clay we didn't move  much but it definitely we didn't move  the percentage of clay  very far but it definitely changed the  the characteristic qualities of the  texture of the soil  so it became more smooth more capable of  forming a longer ribbon  uh with just a little bit more clay and  it changed the  just that small amount of adjustment  changed the soil texture for us in our  diagnostic  this has been a production of cornell